WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.160
You know, usually when we talk about, like, a civilizational crisis, there's this underlying assumption that we are simply running out of things.

00:07.340 --> 00:08.820
Right. The scarcity narrative.

00:09.020 --> 00:09.380
Exactly.

00:09.620 --> 00:09.820
Yeah.

00:09.900 --> 00:19.680
We picture a world where the gas tank is basically hitting empty, like we're running out of clean water, running out of arable land, running out of civic trust, running out of time.

00:19.780 --> 00:21.320
It's always a story of depletion.

00:21.480 --> 00:22.260
Yeah, depletion.

00:22.780 --> 00:28.100
But I want you to imagine for a second that you're in a car, right, and the engine suddenly dies.

00:28.440 --> 00:28.740
Okay.

00:28.740 --> 00:32.240
You look at the fuel gauge, and the tank is completely full.

00:32.840 --> 00:34.240
You haven't run out of gas at all.

00:34.700 --> 00:35.720
The fuel is right there.

00:35.940 --> 00:37.100
But the car won't start.

00:37.320 --> 00:37.520
Right.

00:37.980 --> 00:44.960
Because the fuel line itself, like the actual pathway that connects the resource to the engine, has been systematically dissolved.

00:45.120 --> 00:45.780
Oh, wow.

00:45.980 --> 00:47.200
That's a great way to picture it.

00:47.300 --> 00:47.480
Yeah.

00:47.600 --> 00:49.180
So you're not empty, you know.

00:49.300 --> 00:50.880
You are just disconnected.

00:51.480 --> 00:55.720
And that difference, that is the core of everything we're exploring today.

00:55.760 --> 00:57.160
It completely flips the script.

00:57.480 --> 00:58.140
It really does.

00:58.960 --> 01:09.280
Today, we are taking a sledgehammer to how you think about the polycrisis or the metacrisis or, well, whatever term you use for that overwhelming feeling of modern fragility.

01:09.740 --> 01:11.760
And we have some incredible source material for this.

01:11.820 --> 01:12.140
We do.

01:12.140 --> 01:18.240
Our source material today is a really groundbreaking paper, published today, actually, June 27, 2026.

01:18.580 --> 01:26.500
It's by a theorist named Fliction, and it's titled, The Admissibility Crisis on the Collapse of Future Option Space.

01:26.500 --> 01:28.320
It's a massive shift in perspective.

01:28.320 --> 01:32.560
Yeah, because the argument here is that we have completely misdiagnosed the disease.

01:32.880 --> 01:34.960
Like, we aren't running out of inputs.

01:35.200 --> 01:39.200
We are destroying the actual pathways required to reach a functional future.

01:39.860 --> 01:40.260
Okay.

01:40.360 --> 01:41.120
Let's unpack this.

01:41.120 --> 01:42.660
I'm really glad we're doing this deep dive.

01:42.760 --> 01:47.080
And what I appreciate most about this paper is that the goal isn't to be alarmist.

01:47.180 --> 01:47.360
Right.

01:47.540 --> 01:48.680
No doomsday prophecies.

01:48.800 --> 01:49.240
Exactly.

01:49.380 --> 01:50.880
It doesn't traffic in that kind of stuff.

01:51.040 --> 01:58.620
Instead, Fliction invites us to look at this crisis through a very rigorous geometric and, well, even mathematical lens.

01:58.780 --> 01:59.160
Math.

01:59.880 --> 02:02.200
Which sounds intimidating, but it's actually fascinating.

02:02.200 --> 02:03.040
It is.

02:03.320 --> 02:14.080
We are going to map out the invisible architecture that makes the modern world seem to function so efficiently, yet feel so incredibly brittle to you, to me, and really to everyone else living inside of it.

02:14.180 --> 02:17.340
Yeah, because the paper, I mean, it grabs you by the collar right on page one.

02:17.420 --> 02:17.560
Yeah.

02:17.880 --> 02:22.800
It argues that our entire obsession with scarcity is this giant trap.

02:22.940 --> 02:26.140
A trap that prevents us from seeing the real mechanics of our decline.

02:26.300 --> 02:26.420
Yeah.

02:26.420 --> 02:26.740
Right.

02:27.080 --> 02:32.140
And Fliction introduces this brilliant analogy of a village running out of water.

02:32.860 --> 02:42.400
And I think, well, if we spend some time here, it perfectly captures the difference between a depletion problem and what the paper calls an admissibility problem.

02:42.800 --> 02:46.360
The village analogy is basically the foundation of this entire theoretical framework.

02:46.920 --> 02:48.520
Let's look at the first scenario they present.

02:48.640 --> 02:48.840
Okay.

02:49.000 --> 02:49.740
Set it up for us.

02:50.020 --> 02:50.980
So you have a village.

02:51.120 --> 02:52.480
People have lived there for generations.

02:52.480 --> 02:56.640
And the aquifer beneath it simply drops due to a changing climate.

02:56.980 --> 02:58.200
Just a natural drying out.

02:58.320 --> 02:58.440
Right.

02:58.980 --> 02:59.940
The wells go dry.

03:00.100 --> 03:01.820
The villagers go out with their buckets.

03:01.820 --> 03:03.560
And there is just dust at the bottom.

03:03.720 --> 03:06.040
Eventually, there is literally no water left to pump.

03:06.580 --> 03:08.840
And that is your classic depletion problem.

03:08.960 --> 03:09.440
Exactly.

03:09.680 --> 03:12.680
The resource is physically gone from the geographic space.

03:13.200 --> 03:14.060
The tank is empty.

03:14.820 --> 03:18.740
But then Fliction asks you to imagine a second village.

03:19.320 --> 03:20.580
This is where it gets interesting.

03:21.060 --> 03:21.220
Yeah.

03:21.420 --> 03:24.160
In this second village, the aquifer is completely full.

03:24.580 --> 03:26.300
There's plenty of water beneath the ground.

03:26.300 --> 03:33.520
But the water infrastructure, like the actual piping system, has been privatized by a massive conglomerate.

03:33.520 --> 03:34.940
A very familiar story.

03:35.260 --> 03:35.380
Right.

03:35.460 --> 03:36.640
The new management comes in.

03:37.120 --> 03:42.100
And they optimize the entire system for, well, quarterly throughput and profit margins.

03:42.240 --> 03:43.900
They want the numbers to look good right now.

03:44.060 --> 03:44.400
Exactly.

03:44.680 --> 03:47.900
They look at the ledger and say, you know, we have too many maintenance engineers.

03:48.060 --> 03:51.220
Let's fire half of them to boost short-term efficiency metrics.

03:51.420 --> 03:52.840
Shrinking the repair capacity.

03:52.980 --> 03:53.260
Yes.

03:53.260 --> 03:59.820
And the pipes are redesigned, not for long-term structural integrity or, like, to handle sudden pressure spikes,

03:59.980 --> 04:02.960
but strictly to maximize immediate yield for the shareholders.

04:03.280 --> 04:04.120
So what happens?

04:04.480 --> 04:07.400
Well, five years later, the system suffers a catastrophic breakdown.

04:07.960 --> 04:08.720
The pipes burst.

04:08.880 --> 04:09.680
The pump sees up.

04:10.480 --> 04:13.260
Now, that village also has no water coming out of the taps.

04:13.640 --> 04:15.240
But the resource is still there.

04:15.320 --> 04:15.760
Exactly.

04:16.160 --> 04:17.560
The aquifer is full.

04:17.840 --> 04:18.820
They haven't run out of water.

04:18.900 --> 04:21.520
They've run out of the structural capacity to reach the water.

04:21.520 --> 04:25.700
And that second scenario is what Flixchen calls an admissibility crisis.

04:26.260 --> 04:27.300
Admissibility crisis.

04:27.880 --> 04:31.300
Meaning it's not allowed or not reachable.

04:31.700 --> 04:35.960
Well, the state of having water is no longer admissible or reachable,

04:36.140 --> 04:40.220
given the current structural constraints of the village's infrastructure.

04:40.400 --> 04:40.800
Oh, I see.

04:40.880 --> 04:44.680
What's fascinating here is that to the villagers standing in their kitchens,

04:45.020 --> 04:47.480
the symptoms look completely identical.

04:47.800 --> 04:48.060
Right.

04:48.140 --> 04:49.760
They turn the tap, nothing comes out.

04:49.820 --> 04:50.180
Exactly.

04:50.180 --> 04:53.300
But if they misdiagnose the problem as scarcity,

04:53.640 --> 04:57.220
they will waste all their remaining energy trying to dig deeper wells.

04:57.640 --> 05:00.280
Or they'll just pack up and go looking for entirely new aquifers.

05:00.480 --> 05:02.240
Which solves absolutely nothing.

05:02.400 --> 05:04.540
Because the problem isn't a lack of water.

05:05.000 --> 05:07.480
The problem is the architecture of the pipes.

05:07.760 --> 05:08.200
Okay, wait.

05:08.300 --> 05:09.420
I'm struggling with this a bit.

05:09.880 --> 05:13.440
And I want to push back on how we apply this to the modern world.

05:13.520 --> 05:13.960
Go for it.

05:13.960 --> 05:19.920
If you look at massive corporations today, say like big tech hoarding all the world's data,

05:20.140 --> 05:24.260
or Wall Street firms buying up all the single family homes or farmland,

05:24.620 --> 05:27.740
aren't they literally depleting the resource for the rest of us?

05:27.960 --> 05:28.520
How do you mean?

05:29.000 --> 05:30.560
I mean, they are taking the houses.

05:30.680 --> 05:31.600
They are taking the data.

05:31.960 --> 05:33.380
How is that not scarcity?

05:34.000 --> 05:36.060
The stuff is physically gone from my reach.

05:36.060 --> 05:38.200
That is a great distinction to make.

05:38.360 --> 05:40.960
And it's exactly where affliction wants us to look closer.

05:41.220 --> 05:43.360
Think about the housing example you just brought up.

05:43.440 --> 05:43.600
Okay.

05:43.840 --> 05:45.420
The houses haven't vanished, right?

05:45.680 --> 05:47.860
The physical lumber, the concrete, the roofs,

05:48.020 --> 05:51.000
they are still sitting on the exact same plots of land.

05:51.120 --> 05:51.360
Right.

05:51.600 --> 05:52.960
They didn't take the houses to the moon.

05:53.240 --> 05:53.720
Exactly.

05:54.060 --> 05:56.820
We haven't run out of houses in a physical sense.

05:57.360 --> 06:00.400
What the Wall Street firm has acquired isn't just the asset.

06:00.660 --> 06:04.120
They have acquired the pathway to living in that asset.

06:04.120 --> 06:05.240
Oh, okay.

06:05.300 --> 06:05.940
I'm tracking now.

06:06.160 --> 06:07.820
By controlling the financial infrastructure,

06:08.420 --> 06:11.900
the rental markets, the mortgages, the zoning laws,

06:12.460 --> 06:14.660
they decide who gets to walk through the front door.

06:14.860 --> 06:16.320
And under what economic conditions?

06:16.560 --> 06:17.000
Precisely.

06:17.300 --> 06:19.460
They are governing the reachability of the house.

06:19.860 --> 06:20.420
Oh, okay.

06:20.440 --> 06:20.680
I see.

06:20.980 --> 06:23.880
So it's not about them hoarding the physical atoms of the universe.

06:24.480 --> 06:28.560
It's about them erecting toll booths on the roads that lead to those atoms.

06:28.800 --> 06:30.160
That is a perfect way to put it.

06:30.160 --> 06:34.400
And this fundamentally redefines our understanding of contemporary power.

06:34.620 --> 06:34.940
How so?

06:35.200 --> 06:39.540
Well, the defining problem of our modern economy is not just the concentration of wealth.

06:39.700 --> 06:42.140
It's a concentration of reachability.

06:42.340 --> 06:43.700
The concentration of reachability.

06:43.840 --> 06:44.020
Yeah.

06:44.640 --> 06:51.180
Power today is about governing the conditions under which the underlying assets of our civilization remain usable.

06:51.780 --> 06:55.460
If you control the pathways, you don't even need to own the asset.

06:55.720 --> 06:57.420
You just tax the movement toward it.

06:57.420 --> 06:58.740
That makes total sense.

06:59.460 --> 06:59.980
Okay.

07:00.080 --> 07:04.440
So if the problem isn't the resources themselves, but the paths to get to the future,

07:04.980 --> 07:09.660
we need to basically precisely define what a path and a future actually are in this framework.

07:09.800 --> 07:10.000
Right.

07:10.060 --> 07:10.780
We need definitions.

07:11.160 --> 07:13.040
And Flixion doesn't just use metaphors here.

07:13.400 --> 07:16.460
Like there is actual math, actual geometry involved.

07:16.720 --> 07:19.620
Let's talk about what the paper calls the chessboard of reality.

07:19.940 --> 07:20.120
Yeah.

07:20.180 --> 07:23.620
The paper introduces the concept of a high dimensional state space.

07:23.840 --> 07:25.140
Which sounds very sci-fi.

07:25.140 --> 07:26.120
It does.

07:26.520 --> 07:33.500
But if you're listening, just imagine a vast, multi-dimensional map that contains every single possible configuration of our society.

07:33.740 --> 07:35.440
Like every possible timeline.

07:35.520 --> 07:36.180
Kind of, yeah.

07:36.260 --> 07:42.740
Every way our institutions, our ecology, our laws, and our material world could possibly be arranged.

07:43.120 --> 07:49.580
And Flixion defines two crucial geometric concepts to navigate the space admissibility and reachability.

07:49.740 --> 07:50.880
Okay, let's break those down.

07:51.440 --> 07:52.880
What exactly is admissibility?

07:52.880 --> 07:58.440
Admissibility is represented mathematically as V sub A, which stands for the admissibility volume.

07:58.500 --> 07:59.340
V sub A, okay.

07:59.800 --> 08:06.500
A state or a future is admissible if a system can coherently occupy it while maintaining its foundational distinctions.

08:06.960 --> 08:08.220
Wait, plain English, please.

08:08.640 --> 08:12.100
In plain English, it just means the system doesn't break its own rules to exist.

08:12.260 --> 08:12.820
Oh, okay.

08:12.820 --> 08:17.880
It doesn't mean the state is morally good or economically efficient or fair to the people in it.

08:17.940 --> 08:19.580
It just means it's structurally coherent.

08:20.360 --> 08:28.120
The paper uses the concept of a boundary operator, denoted as B of T, to describe the rules or constraints that keep a system coherent.

08:28.120 --> 08:36.020
So if we look at the chess analogy the paper uses, a position on a chess board can be completely legal.

08:36.300 --> 08:37.520
That makes it admissible, right?

08:37.560 --> 08:38.040
Exactly.

08:38.300 --> 08:39.980
Like a knight is on a square.

08:40.200 --> 08:41.940
It's mathematically allowed to be on.

08:42.120 --> 08:44.080
The pawns haven't moved backward.

08:44.500 --> 08:48.240
The king isn't in check in some logically impossible way.

08:48.240 --> 08:51.640
The board state obeys the foundational rules of chess.

08:52.060 --> 08:52.180
Right.

08:52.320 --> 08:58.340
So that specific arrangement of pieces is an admissible state within the universe of the game.

08:58.520 --> 09:00.300
That's a perfect encapsulation.

09:00.920 --> 09:04.240
But here is the critical distinction that drives the rest of the paper.

09:05.040 --> 09:08.460
Just because a state is admissible doesn't mean it is reachable.

09:08.840 --> 09:09.080
Okay.

09:09.160 --> 09:09.980
What is reachability?

09:10.240 --> 09:16.760
Reachability, represented by the variable R, refers to the states you can actually get to from your current position,

09:16.760 --> 09:20.240
using the moves or interventions you currently have available.

09:20.620 --> 09:24.760
Let me throw out another analogy here to see if I'm tracking, because I play a lot of video games.

09:24.820 --> 09:25.480
I'd love to hear it.

09:25.640 --> 09:30.180
This sounds exactly like when you're playing an open-world role-playing game, and there is a glitch.

09:30.480 --> 09:31.500
Oh, I see where you're going.

09:31.840 --> 09:32.140
Right.

09:32.560 --> 09:36.680
Like you can physically see the final boss's castle on the horizon.

09:37.000 --> 09:39.280
The castle exists in the game's code.

09:39.640 --> 09:41.480
It's a legal admissible location.

09:41.780 --> 09:42.820
But you can't get there.

09:43.040 --> 09:43.440
Exactly.

09:43.440 --> 09:49.000
Because a bridge collapsed earlier in your playthrough, or a quest line bugged out, so there's an invisible wall.

09:49.300 --> 09:54.980
You can walk back and forth all day, but you cannot physically traverse the space to get to the castle.

09:55.160 --> 09:56.080
That is brilliant.

09:56.480 --> 10:02.420
So the future where you fight the boss is admissible, but totally unreachable from your current save file.

10:02.500 --> 10:02.780
Yes.

10:03.220 --> 10:07.100
The castle is in the set V sub A, but it is not in the set R.

10:07.320 --> 10:07.860
Wow, okay.

10:07.860 --> 10:15.220
And conversely, to stick with gaming or chess, you can't just forcefully optimize your way into an illegal state to win.

10:15.520 --> 10:15.920
What do you mean?

10:16.220 --> 10:20.740
Well, in chess, you can't say, well, the most efficient way to win is to move my rook diagonally.

10:20.860 --> 10:22.620
Because rooks can't move diagonally.

10:22.800 --> 10:23.240
Exactly.

10:23.500 --> 10:26.300
If you move a rook diagonally, you aren't playing chess anymore.

10:26.420 --> 10:27.840
You just broke the boundary operator.

10:28.240 --> 10:30.480
The game literally ceases to exist.

10:30.560 --> 10:35.080
So if you break the rules of the system to achieve a goal, the system itself collapses.

10:35.080 --> 10:41.100
Exactly. And what's fascinating here is that admissibility is strictly prior to preference.

10:41.440 --> 10:41.980
Prior to preference.

10:42.260 --> 10:54.380
Meaning you cannot simply vote or buy or optimize your way to a specific future if the underlying structural constraints, that boundary operator B of T, have collapsed.

10:54.420 --> 10:58.080
Oh, I get it. It doesn't matter how badly society wants something.

10:58.420 --> 11:01.760
If the physics of the system no longer support it, it's off the board.

11:01.780 --> 11:03.260
It's completely off the board.

11:03.260 --> 11:07.420
Can you ground that boundary operator concept in something real?

11:07.920 --> 11:12.200
Like, what does it look like when the rules of the game collapse in actual human society?

11:12.880 --> 11:15.760
Think about the foundational constraints of a functioning democracy.

11:15.980 --> 11:16.240
Okay.

11:16.400 --> 11:23.220
For a democracy to be structurally coherent, for it to be admissible, certain structural distinctions have to be maintained.

11:23.500 --> 11:23.860
Like what?

11:24.100 --> 11:27.660
Well, there has to be a distinction between public service and private enrichment.

11:27.940 --> 11:31.080
There has to be a distinction between legal evidence and pure fabrication.

11:31.300 --> 11:32.120
Right. Basic facts.

11:32.120 --> 11:38.660
Exactly. There must be epistemic standards, meaning a shared bedrock agreement on how we establish what is true.

11:38.780 --> 11:40.080
And if we lose those.

11:40.380 --> 11:54.160
If those distinctions degrade like, if news becomes indistinguishable from paid propaganda, or if the justice system becomes entirely transactional, the boundary operator of the democracy collapses.

11:54.160 --> 11:56.020
That sounds terrifyingly familiar.

11:56.020 --> 12:02.400
When that happens, the total volume of possible, coherent democratic futures literally shrinks.

12:02.700 --> 12:07.660
You can still hold elections, but the democracy is essentially playing with rooks moving diagonally.

12:07.920 --> 12:10.180
The system has lost its structural integrity.

12:10.340 --> 12:11.220
The board gets smaller.

12:11.220 --> 12:11.700
Yes.

12:11.700 --> 12:12.860
The board gets smaller.

12:13.180 --> 12:16.580
And the same applies to something as fundamental as a language.

12:16.880 --> 12:17.320
How so?

12:17.540 --> 12:19.040
A language requires speakers.

12:19.240 --> 12:20.200
It requires a community.

12:20.460 --> 12:23.340
It requires transmission mechanisms from elders to children.

12:23.560 --> 12:23.760
Right.

12:23.820 --> 12:24.840
You have to be able to pass it on.

12:24.980 --> 12:33.840
If those transmission mechanisms are destroyed, say, by forced assimilation policies or the collapse of a local community, the language crosses a threshold.

12:33.840 --> 12:34.920
And then it's just gone.

12:35.800 --> 12:43.940
At that point, recovering the language might be conceptually coherent in principle, but it is completely unreachable from the current state of reality.

12:44.320 --> 12:49.940
The sweet spot that governs a flourishing civilization is the intersection of these two concepts.

12:50.520 --> 12:53.520
The admissible, reachable set, or R sub A.

12:53.640 --> 12:55.120
The admissible, reachable set.

12:55.280 --> 12:55.520
Oh.

12:55.520 --> 13:05.740
So the futures that are both structurally sound, where the rules haven't broken down, and these that we actually have the pathways to reach from where we stand today.

13:05.860 --> 13:06.860
That is exactly it.

13:06.960 --> 13:15.500
And the terrifying core thesis of this paper is that this set, R sub A, is actively shrinking.

13:16.040 --> 13:17.140
We are losing options.

13:17.380 --> 13:18.460
Systematically losing them.

13:18.460 --> 13:24.800
I really want you, the listener, to just pause and think about the institutions you interact with every single day.

13:24.800 --> 13:26.000
That's a great exercise.

13:26.160 --> 13:30.160
Like, if you're listening to this on a commute right now, think about the infrastructure you're traveling on.

13:30.420 --> 13:34.940
Think about your local city council, your healthcare network, the digital platforms on your phone.

13:35.200 --> 13:36.640
Are they expanding your options?

13:36.880 --> 13:38.000
Are they building new bridges?

13:38.580 --> 13:42.880
Or are they actively shrinking the board of possible futures you can play on?

13:43.140 --> 13:47.400
And that reflection leads us directly to the most unsettling question, Infliction's paper.

13:47.580 --> 13:47.940
Which is?

13:47.940 --> 13:55.960
If we are systematically shrinking our own option space, if we are actively destroying our own pathways to the future, why are we doing it?

13:56.080 --> 13:56.320
Right.

13:56.560 --> 14:05.200
Is there some smoke-filled room of evil masterminds at the top pulling the strings, twirling their mustaches, and saying, you know, let's destroy the future?

14:05.440 --> 14:07.740
Because it certainly feels like someone must be to blame.

14:08.000 --> 14:10.260
It's a natural human instinct to look for a villain.

14:10.900 --> 14:12.220
We want someone to point at.

14:12.380 --> 14:13.020
Yeah, of course.

14:13.020 --> 14:18.420
But the paper argues something much more profound and honestly much more difficult to fix.

14:19.120 --> 14:20.980
The crisis is not driven by malice.

14:21.380 --> 14:23.940
It is driven by local rationality.

14:24.260 --> 14:25.440
Local rationality.

14:25.540 --> 14:25.680
Yeah.

14:25.760 --> 14:31.560
Meaning everyone is just doing what makes perfect sense for them in their specific little bubble, given the incentives they face.

14:31.820 --> 14:32.340
Precisely.

14:32.700 --> 14:35.180
Let's look at the mechanisms driving our world right now.

14:35.520 --> 14:38.660
Take engagement maximization on social media platforms.

14:38.840 --> 14:39.540
Okay, the algorithms.

14:39.820 --> 14:42.040
The engineers writing those algorithms aren't evil.

14:42.040 --> 14:44.360
They are facing a quarterly earnings review.

14:45.100 --> 14:53.400
It is perfectly locally rational for them to tweak the algorithm to keep you on screen for an extra five minutes, because that's what their bonus is tied to.

14:53.760 --> 14:54.760
So it makes sense for them.

14:54.880 --> 14:55.340
Exactly.

14:55.860 --> 14:58.380
Or look at algorithmic labor management.

14:58.820 --> 15:08.120
When a massive logistics company uses software to track its delivery drivers down to the exact second, penalizing them for taking a bathroom break.

15:08.240 --> 15:08.460
Right.

15:08.540 --> 15:10.580
The warehouse horrors we hear about.

15:10.580 --> 15:14.840
The middle managers implementing that software aren't sadists.

15:15.280 --> 15:20.040
They are working for a company whose singular goal is minimizing supply chain variance.

15:20.560 --> 15:25.260
It is entirely rational for them to squeeze the human element out of the process.

15:25.260 --> 15:27.460
And the same goes for planned obsolescence.

15:27.880 --> 15:28.120
Right.

15:28.560 --> 15:34.320
Designing a washing machine or a smartphone with a glued-in battery so it breaks in three years instead of lasting 20.

15:34.520 --> 15:34.860
Yes.

15:35.000 --> 15:35.780
A perfect example.

15:36.000 --> 15:40.920
The CEO of that manufacturing company needs continuous consumer demand to keep the stock price up.

15:41.080 --> 15:43.460
If they build a fund that lasts forever, they go out of business.

15:43.980 --> 15:46.400
So it's completely rational for them to build junk.

15:46.640 --> 15:47.080
Exactly.

15:47.080 --> 15:49.220
None of these people are irrational.

15:49.700 --> 15:55.600
None of them necessarily want to destroy society's long-term option space or immiserate the working class.

15:55.800 --> 15:56.800
They're just doing their jobs.

15:57.220 --> 16:00.980
They are just optimizing for their specific, narrowly defined metric.

16:00.980 --> 16:08.240
But the aggregate result of all this perfectly logical local optimization is a global catastrophe.

16:08.880 --> 16:08.900
Okay.

16:09.000 --> 16:11.640
This is where we have to talk about how fliction models this.

16:12.200 --> 16:20.840
Because the paper introduces a mathematical concept called the carelessness principle to explain how this aggregate destruction happens without malice.

16:21.100 --> 16:21.340
Yes.

16:21.680 --> 16:24.420
The carelessness principle models systemic externalization.

16:25.160 --> 16:29.560
It's a way of showing how systems become totally blind to the damage they cause.

16:29.620 --> 16:30.360
Let's get into the math.

16:30.360 --> 16:33.460
In calculus, a partial derivative measures sensitivity.

16:34.300 --> 16:38.060
It asks, how much does one thing change when another thing changes?

16:38.160 --> 16:38.320
Okay.

16:38.400 --> 16:39.500
I remember that from high school.

16:39.820 --> 16:46.860
Fliction sets up a relationship between an optimization metric, let's call it m, and a real-world variable, let's call it x.

16:47.200 --> 16:52.560
The math states that the partial derivative of m with respect to x is approximately 0.

16:53.160 --> 16:57.460
Or partial m over partial x is approximately 0.

16:57.460 --> 17:00.920
Let's slow down and ground this, because this is the hinge of the entire argument, I think.

17:01.160 --> 17:05.840
The optimization metric, m, is whatever the system is desperately trying to maximize, right?

17:05.840 --> 17:05.960
Sure.

17:06.060 --> 17:07.360
It could be quarterly profit.

17:07.500 --> 17:08.640
It could be user engagement.

17:08.780 --> 17:10.020
It could be clicks on an ad.

17:10.020 --> 17:22.560
And the variable x is something else entirely in the real world, like the ecological health of a river, or the mental well-being of a teenager, or the institutional memory of a local library.

17:22.560 --> 17:37.120
Perfect. So, if the partial derivative is 0, if partial m over partial x is approximately 0, it means the system's metric, m, does not change when the real-world variable x changes.

17:37.340 --> 17:38.540
Give me a concrete example.

17:38.540 --> 17:41.780
If the river gets polluted, the profit metric doesn't drop.

17:42.220 --> 17:46.460
If the teenager gets depressed, the engagement metric doesn't register a failure.

17:47.000 --> 17:49.800
The system's metric is entirely blind to that variable.

17:50.300 --> 17:52.940
The system structurally does not care about x.

17:53.020 --> 17:54.900
And it's not care in an emotional sense.

17:55.020 --> 17:56.160
The algorithm doesn't have a heart.

17:56.480 --> 17:57.680
It's mathematical blindness.

17:58.600 --> 18:04.320
I love the analogy the paper uses here, and I want to expand on it, because it really clarifies that this isn't about human malice.

18:04.560 --> 18:05.260
The truck analogy.

18:05.260 --> 18:08.260
Yes. Think about a massive semi-truck driving down a highway.

18:08.700 --> 18:13.540
As the truck moves forward, carrying its cargo, the heavy tires create immense friction.

18:13.800 --> 18:15.440
They slowly wear down the asphalt.

18:15.800 --> 18:19.120
The truck consumes the road surface just by existing and moving.

18:20.160 --> 18:22.480
Now, does the truck hate the road?

18:22.800 --> 18:26.220
Of course not. The truck has no feelings toward the road whatsoever.

18:26.440 --> 18:28.180
No. The truck isn't malicious.

18:28.680 --> 18:30.460
The truck is completely indifferent to the road.

18:30.460 --> 18:36.320
Its only goal, its optimization metric, M, is forward motion to deliver the cargo on time.

18:36.920 --> 18:38.480
The destruction is just a byproduct.

18:38.960 --> 18:41.960
The destruction of the road surface is just a byproduct of that motion.

18:42.500 --> 18:47.560
The truck's dashboard doesn't have a gauge for, like, structural integrity of the asphalt.

18:48.420 --> 18:51.360
The system is blind to the substrate it drives on.

18:51.360 --> 18:54.240
That is a brilliant expansion of the carelessness principle.

18:54.800 --> 18:56.780
The indifference is entirely structural.

18:57.220 --> 19:03.180
And at a civilizational scale, systemic indifference is actually far more destructive than active hostility.

19:03.300 --> 19:05.400
Wait, really? More destructive than hostility?

19:05.520 --> 19:05.920
Absolutely.

19:06.180 --> 19:07.300
Hostility is visible.

19:07.620 --> 19:09.420
Hostility generates political resistance.

19:09.420 --> 19:14.360
If a dictator says, I'm going to destroy this forest, people can chain themselves to the trees.

19:14.680 --> 19:15.840
Oh, I see. You can fight it.

19:16.080 --> 19:17.080
But indifference.

19:17.680 --> 19:23.880
Indifference just quietly consumes its substrate in the background until the substrate collapses beneath our feet.

19:24.660 --> 19:30.340
The paper provides two incredible case studies to illustrate this dynamic in the real world.

19:31.000 --> 19:32.600
Let's look at the smartphone industry.

19:32.760 --> 19:34.560
Let's do it. I mean, we all have one in our pocket.

19:34.720 --> 19:34.980
Yeah.

19:35.100 --> 19:38.260
And I think we all have the shared frustration of these devices breaking.

19:38.260 --> 19:47.320
My own phone, I swear, exactly a month after the two-year warranty expired, a software update essentially bricked the battery life.

19:47.580 --> 19:49.840
It's infuriating, but it's part of the design.

19:49.980 --> 19:55.720
Now, we know these devices are optimized for profit margins, unit volume, and quick replacement cycles.

19:56.160 --> 19:57.320
That is their metric, Em.

19:57.480 --> 19:57.740
Right.

19:57.960 --> 20:01.000
But what is left outside that optimization metric?

20:01.120 --> 20:03.500
What is the X that they are mathematically blind to?

20:03.500 --> 20:08.660
They are blind to the substrate, the physical and social reality upon which the device is actually built.

20:08.800 --> 20:09.400
The real world.

20:09.660 --> 20:10.140
Exactly.

20:10.640 --> 20:18.940
The optimization metric of the massive manufacturer does not measure the brutal labor conditions in the overseas factories where those tiny components are assembled.

20:18.960 --> 20:19.700
It doesn't see them.

20:19.940 --> 20:27.040
It doesn't measure the toxic chemical runoff from the recycling processes where your old, bricked phone is eventually dumped.

20:27.040 --> 20:32.180
It doesn't measure the staggering ecological cost of strip mining rare earth metals.

20:32.460 --> 20:34.440
Because none of that hurts the profit margin.

20:34.700 --> 20:40.260
Because those massive human and environmental costs do not show up on the company's quarterly balance sheet.

20:40.580 --> 20:42.480
The system is mathematically blind to them.

20:42.820 --> 20:46.240
The true costs of the device are completely externalized.

20:46.240 --> 20:51.640
So the device is incredibly cheap for us to buy and wildly profitable for them to sell.

20:52.600 --> 20:56.600
Precisely because the system doesn't have to pay for the damage it does to the substrate.

20:56.720 --> 20:58.300
It's the truck consuming the road.

20:58.740 --> 21:01.840
And the second case study the paper brings up is even wilder.

21:01.980 --> 21:05.840
Because it shows what happens when the reality starts to fight back against the metric.

21:06.280 --> 21:08.400
Let's talk about industrial meat production.

21:08.400 --> 21:13.260
This case study highlights a different, deeply insidious aspect of the trap.

21:13.680 --> 21:21.040
Factory farming, the ultra-optimized, hyper-dense raising of livestock, generates massive admissibility costs.

21:21.160 --> 21:22.520
Costs that threaten our system.

21:22.800 --> 21:27.140
Yes, these are things that actively threaten our society's structural coherence.

21:27.660 --> 21:33.760
We are talking about the rapid accumulation of antibiotic-resistant bacteria in the global microbiome

21:33.760 --> 21:38.800
because they pump the animals full of drugs just to keep them alive in those conditions.

21:38.900 --> 21:40.300
Which is terrifying for public health.

21:40.480 --> 21:42.700
We are talking about massive methane emissions.

21:43.260 --> 21:48.980
We are talking about local water systems completely stressed and poisoned by agricultural runoff.

21:49.120 --> 21:50.560
These aren't minor issues.

21:50.920 --> 21:54.940
These are existential threats to human health and ecological stability.

21:55.120 --> 21:55.520
Absolutely.

21:55.520 --> 22:03.200
And the massive conglomerates running these factory farms know that if the public really understood the scale of these costs,

22:03.840 --> 22:04.780
demand would plummet.

22:05.160 --> 22:06.340
Regulators would step in.

22:06.460 --> 22:07.220
So what do they do?

22:07.360 --> 22:08.940
Do they fix the underlying reality?

22:09.380 --> 22:13.300
Do they redesign the farms to be ecologically sound and repair the substrate?

22:13.600 --> 22:13.880
No.

22:14.200 --> 22:18.520
Because repairing the reality would fundamentally reduce their optimization metric profit.

22:18.800 --> 22:19.880
Because it's less efficient.

22:20.140 --> 22:22.220
Ecological farming is less hyper-efficient.

22:22.220 --> 22:28.860
So instead of repairing the reality, the industry spends billions of dollars repairing the representation of the reality.

22:29.480 --> 22:32.220
They launch massive, sophisticated marketing campaigns,

22:33.200 --> 22:38.080
showing happy cows grazing on pristine green hillsides with a whistling farmer.

22:38.320 --> 22:39.180
Which doesn't exist.

22:39.500 --> 22:43.440
They fund industry-friendly research to confuse the nutritional science.

22:43.680 --> 22:49.580
They lobby to pass ag-gag laws that make it a literal crime for journalists to film inside the facilities.

22:49.580 --> 22:52.160
So they are repairing the map, not the territory.

22:52.720 --> 22:58.520
They are fixing the PR image so the metric can keep growing while the actual physical reality rots.

22:58.520 --> 22:59.200
Exactly.

22:59.940 --> 23:06.040
The resources, the capital, and the organizational competence for repair absolutely exist within these companies.

23:06.760 --> 23:13.580
But the gradient of their optimization metric directs all that repair energy entirely toward the public relations image,

23:13.920 --> 23:18.160
the representation, while the underlying reality continues to deteriorate.

23:18.160 --> 23:23.840
You know, this raises an incredible point about the whole concept of corporate social responsibility campaigns, doesn't it?

23:23.880 --> 23:24.680
It really does.

23:24.760 --> 23:30.440
We look at these companies, whether it's big oil or fast fashion or industrial ag, and we demand they do better.

23:30.620 --> 23:31.440
We sign petitions.

23:31.960 --> 23:33.540
We try to shame them into caring.

23:34.040 --> 23:38.560
And Fliction argues, with mathematical precision, that this is fundamentally futile.

23:38.560 --> 23:45.300
You cannot shame an optimization landscape into caring about a variable it mathematically cannot see.

23:45.540 --> 23:46.620
Because the derivative is zero.

23:46.880 --> 23:50.820
You are trying to use moral arguments to change a structural derivative.

23:51.180 --> 23:51.860
It won't work.

23:52.620 --> 23:57.780
The system isn't indifferent because the executives are inherently evil people who want to destroy the earth.

23:58.000 --> 24:05.720
The system is indifferent because the metric they are legally and financially required to optimize is entirely blind to the substrate.

24:05.720 --> 24:08.920
So until the metric changes, the behavior cannot change.

24:09.860 --> 24:14.420
Which leads us perfectly into one of the deepest, most mind-bending parts of this deep dive.

24:15.080 --> 24:22.560
If we are constantly repairing the representation of reality instead of reality itself, what happens when the representation totally takes over?

24:22.740 --> 24:23.900
That is the big question.

24:24.240 --> 24:27.340
What happens when the map becomes more powerful than the territory?

24:28.300 --> 24:31.460
Fliction weaves together two classic theorists here to explain this.

24:32.120 --> 24:33.760
Jacques Elluz and Jean Baudrillard.

24:33.760 --> 24:36.840
This is where we get into what the paper calls the compression trap.

24:37.380 --> 24:43.760
It's a brilliant synthesis of Elluz's concept of technique and Baudrillard's concept of hyper-reality.

24:44.540 --> 24:46.980
Okay, let's take those one by one because these are heavy hitters.

24:48.020 --> 24:49.100
Jacques Elluz.

24:49.600 --> 24:52.060
Who was he and what did he mean by technique?

24:52.060 --> 24:57.480
Jacques Elluz was a mid-20th century French philosopher and sociologist

24:57.480 --> 25:05.680
who observed that modern societies tend to reorganize themselves around one supreme value maximum efficiency.

25:05.820 --> 25:07.000
Efficiency above all else.

25:07.060 --> 25:09.520
He called this overarching drive la technique.

25:10.360 --> 25:13.880
Crucially, he wasn't just being a Luddite saying technology is bad.

25:14.000 --> 25:16.340
He was pointing out a structural logic.

25:16.560 --> 25:16.760
Okay.

25:16.760 --> 25:25.320
He argued that once you make efficiency the absolute dominant goal of an institution, that institution starts selecting only for things that can be quantified and optimized.

25:25.320 --> 25:30.080
By necessity, it systematically eliminates anything that cannot be optimized.

25:30.360 --> 25:38.320
So if a future is messy or beautiful or relies on deep, slow human relationships, but it can't be put into a spreadsheet and maximized,

25:39.040 --> 25:40.480
well, the system just prunes it away.

25:40.700 --> 25:41.460
It views it as friction.

25:41.460 --> 25:42.380
Exactly.

25:43.020 --> 25:46.820
Technique introduces a ruthless selective pressure on future states.

25:47.500 --> 25:49.400
The system gains incredible local performance.

25:49.640 --> 25:55.680
It gets very fast, very predictable, and very cheap, but it loses global optionality.

25:56.060 --> 25:57.360
So what does this all mean?

25:57.480 --> 25:59.280
I have to play devil's advocate here for a second.

25:59.300 --> 25:59.680
Please do.

25:59.960 --> 26:02.360
Because when I hear efficiency, I think that's a good thing.

26:02.920 --> 26:05.040
Isn't optimization exactly what we want?

26:05.040 --> 26:11.000
If my mom has to go into the hospital for surgery, I don't want a messy, unoptimized hospital.

26:11.260 --> 26:14.200
I want that hospital to be a perfectly oiled machine.

26:14.500 --> 26:14.580
Right.

26:14.660 --> 26:15.520
You want the best care.

26:15.640 --> 26:17.340
I want the nurses to be efficient.

26:17.860 --> 26:21.200
I want the supply chain for the anesthesia to be perfectly optimized.

26:21.400 --> 26:22.780
So why is this bad?

26:23.340 --> 26:27.060
It's the most common defense of the current system, and it's a great question.

26:27.600 --> 26:30.280
Liction addresses this directly using the hospital analogy.

26:30.580 --> 26:34.160
Let's look closely at what optimization actually means in modern health care.

26:34.160 --> 26:39.080
A hospital that is perfectly optimized for three-put, meaning it maximizes the number

26:39.080 --> 26:40.460
of patients seen per hour.

26:40.700 --> 26:42.540
It minimizes the time beds are empty.

26:42.900 --> 26:48.160
It runs a just-in-time supply chain for PPE to keep storage costs at absolute zero.

26:48.980 --> 26:53.920
That hospital is brilliant at handling predictable, standard cases.

26:54.120 --> 26:55.140
It sounds great on paper.

26:55.360 --> 26:57.620
It looks incredibly efficient on a spreadsheet.

26:58.200 --> 27:01.720
The shareholders or the regional health board will applaud the administrators.

27:01.720 --> 27:08.380
But that exact same hyper-optimization makes the hospital completely brittle to unusual cases

27:08.380 --> 27:11.700
or to a sudden, unpredictable, systemic shock.

27:12.020 --> 27:12.540
Like a pandemic.

27:12.660 --> 27:13.340
Like a pandemic.

27:13.340 --> 27:18.600
Because all the slack in the system, the empty beds waiting just in case, the stockpiles of

27:18.600 --> 27:23.640
masks sitting in a closet unused, the extra nurses on shift who might have downtime, all

27:23.640 --> 27:26.840
of that slack was viewed by the optimization metric as inefficiency.

27:27.320 --> 27:27.880
Oh, man.

27:28.360 --> 27:29.040
So they cut it.

27:29.200 --> 27:30.460
It was pruned away by technique.

27:30.460 --> 27:30.980
Oh, wow.

27:31.280 --> 27:32.400
I never thought about it like that.

27:32.780 --> 27:35.540
Efficiency is just the elimination of preparation for the unknown.

27:35.980 --> 27:36.540
Precisely.

27:37.180 --> 27:43.320
To be perfectly optimized for the known, predictable present means aggressively eliminating the

27:43.320 --> 27:47.020
redundant capacity you need to survive an unknown future.

27:47.400 --> 27:52.400
We gain local performance on Tuesday, but we lose global optionality for the decade.

27:52.700 --> 27:54.140
The admissibility volume shrinks?

27:54.520 --> 27:58.360
The admissibility volume shrinks because the system has no shock absorbers left.

27:58.360 --> 28:02.120
Okay, so that's Elul and the hidden danger of hyperoptimization.

28:02.680 --> 28:04.680
Now, let's bring in Jean Baudrillard.

28:04.880 --> 28:08.100
He's the philosopher famous for talking about the map and the territory, right?

28:08.180 --> 28:09.940
The idea of hyperreality.

28:10.180 --> 28:10.460
Yes.

28:11.220 --> 28:16.740
Baudrillard's central concern was what happens to our representations of reality in a media-saturated,

28:16.880 --> 28:18.140
hyperoptimized world.

28:18.340 --> 28:19.200
So what does happen?

28:19.200 --> 28:24.360
He argued that eventually the sign or the metric detaches entirely from the real world

28:24.360 --> 28:25.960
thing it is supposed to signify.

28:26.380 --> 28:30.140
And eventually the representation becomes more causally powerful than the real thing

28:30.140 --> 28:30.520
itself.

28:30.720 --> 28:32.980
The map becomes more important than the territory.

28:33.180 --> 28:36.960
Let's give a concrete example of this compression trap, because this feels very abstract.

28:37.740 --> 28:40.160
How do we compress reality and how does it trap us?

28:40.160 --> 28:47.060
The classic macroeconomic example the paper uses is gross domestic product, or GDP.

28:47.660 --> 28:48.700
Ah, GDP.

28:48.920 --> 28:53.580
Originally, when GDP was developed in the mid-20th century, it was just a useful compression

28:53.580 --> 28:53.960
tool.

28:54.120 --> 28:54.880
It was a map.

28:55.100 --> 28:56.160
A way to measure things.

28:56.220 --> 28:56.440
Right.

28:56.660 --> 29:02.680
It took a vastly complex, incredibly messy economic reality, millions of people trading,

29:03.340 --> 29:09.400
building, growing food, caring for children, and compressed all of that into a single tractable

29:09.400 --> 29:13.180
number so policymakers could try to get a rough understanding of what was happening.

29:13.320 --> 29:13.520
Right.

29:13.560 --> 29:14.800
It was just a descriptive tool.

29:14.900 --> 29:15.860
It described the economy.

29:15.860 --> 29:20.080
But then, over time, the institutions start to organize around the metric.

29:20.560 --> 29:23.520
Politicians are elected or fired based on whether GDP goes up.

29:24.000 --> 29:26.840
Bureaucrats build their entire careers on manipulating it.

29:27.280 --> 29:29.560
Laws are passed specifically to boost it.

29:29.660 --> 29:30.960
It becomes the goal itself.

29:31.300 --> 29:36.460
Eventually, the representation GDP acquires more operational force than the real economy

29:36.460 --> 29:37.320
was supposed to measure.

29:37.320 --> 29:41.280
This reminds me perfectly of how schools shifted to standardized testing.

29:41.400 --> 29:42.520
Oh, that's a great comparison.

29:43.040 --> 29:46.300
The original goal of a standardized test was just to measure learning.

29:47.100 --> 29:48.180
The test was the map.

29:48.700 --> 29:52.340
The child's actual education and critical thinking skills were the territory.

29:52.600 --> 29:52.860
Right.

29:53.100 --> 29:57.620
But once school funding and teacher salaries were tied to the test scores, the school stopped

29:57.620 --> 30:00.620
caring about the broad, unmeasurable territory of education.

30:01.000 --> 30:02.060
They just taught to the test.

30:02.200 --> 30:02.880
They cut art.

30:03.000 --> 30:03.540
They cut recess.

30:03.680 --> 30:06.100
They cut deep reading, all to optimize the metric.

30:06.100 --> 30:08.120
The test ate the education.

30:08.500 --> 30:13.580
That is a phenomenal original analogy, and it maps flawlessly onto Fliction's math.

30:13.700 --> 30:19.900
The paper literally provides an equation for this exact phenomenon, x prime equals f of

30:19.900 --> 30:21.220
x comma pi of x.

30:21.320 --> 30:21.780
Let me guess.

30:22.080 --> 30:24.940
X is the reality, and pi of x is the map.

30:25.060 --> 30:25.540
Exactly.

30:25.980 --> 30:30.800
The math shows that the actual system x becomes a function of its own compressed representation.

30:30.980 --> 30:32.360
The causal error reverses.

30:32.500 --> 30:34.060
The map controls the territory.

30:34.060 --> 30:36.020
It's not just that the map is more important.

30:36.400 --> 30:40.140
It's that the territory actively reorganizes itself to fit the map.

30:40.540 --> 30:46.500
So in the GDP example, we start enacting policies that artificially inflate the GDP, number like

30:46.500 --> 30:52.440
deregulating harmful industries, or promoting speculative financial bubbles, or forcing people

30:52.440 --> 31:01.680
to pay for things they used to share for free, even if those policies actively destroy the underlying health of the real economy, and make everyone more miserable.

31:01.840 --> 31:04.600
Because the reality reshapes itself to fit the number.

31:04.920 --> 31:05.220
Wow.

31:05.340 --> 31:09.000
And Fliction points out this pathology is happening everywhere in our modern lives.

31:09.000 --> 31:10.420
Look at social media.

31:10.420 --> 31:18.820
Originally, it was a tool to connect people, but the platform has compressed human connection into a metric called engagement, or time on site.

31:18.940 --> 31:20.520
So reality reshaped itself again.

31:20.600 --> 31:26.420
The algorithms realized outrage and extreme polarization keep people clicking longer than nuanced agreement.

31:26.880 --> 31:35.200
The territory of human social interaction reorganized itself to fit the metric of engagement, destroying actual social cohesion in the process.

31:35.280 --> 31:36.900
Or look at how we measure expertise.

31:36.900 --> 31:39.860
It used to be based on deep knowledge and peer respect.

31:40.380 --> 31:42.960
Now follower counts have replaced actual expertise.

31:43.120 --> 31:45.760
You aren't an expert because you know things.

31:46.420 --> 31:47.720
You are considered an expert.

31:47.980 --> 31:51.760
You get the book deals, you get the TV spots, because you have the metric of an expert.

31:52.220 --> 31:55.260
The paper even points out this is happening in hard science, right?

31:55.320 --> 31:55.880
Oh, absolutely.

31:56.340 --> 32:00.440
Citation counts in academia are replacing actual scientific understanding.

32:00.440 --> 32:10.440
Researchers chop their work into tiny, salami-sliced papers just to maximize their citation metric, rather than pursuing deep, risky, unoptimizable breakthroughs.

32:11.160 --> 32:13.400
It is the defining pathology of our time.

32:14.140 --> 32:18.120
Institutions navigate by the gradient of the metric, not by the reality.

32:18.260 --> 32:27.480
And the harder they optimize for the metric, the further their trajectories drift from the actual social, educational, or ecological reality they were originally built to serve.

32:27.480 --> 32:30.480
It's terrifying when you realize how pervasive it is.

32:30.560 --> 32:32.020
You can see it in every single industry.

32:32.760 --> 32:38.620
But, you know, the map eating the territory is dangerous enough when it's just a local school board or a single company doing it.

32:39.060 --> 32:47.400
What happens when the actual infrastructure that produces these maps, the systems that define these metrics for the entire globe, are owned by just a few massive entities?

32:47.400 --> 32:54.780
This brings us to a crucial pivot in the paper, where Fliction explores how contemporary power enforces this compression.

32:55.680 --> 33:02.520
We are moving beyond just saying metrics are bad and looking closely at the literal architecture of the platforms themselves.

33:02.520 --> 33:20.920
The paper introduces this idea of platforms as distinction governors, and it argues that all our usual everyday critiques of big tech-like, that they are monopolies, that their algorithms are politically biased, that they censor people, those critiques totally miss the deeper structural point.

33:20.960 --> 33:21.620
They really do.

33:22.080 --> 33:28.800
Historically, society relied on a vast distributed network of institutions to repair what Fliction calls distinctions.

33:29.060 --> 33:29.840
What kind of distinctions?

33:29.840 --> 33:39.160
A society needs to maintain distinctions to function, the distinction between truth and falsehood, between public and private, between legitimate expertise and charlatanism.

33:39.380 --> 33:42.860
And historically, we had a whole ecosystem for this, like local libraries.

33:43.440 --> 33:45.940
Libraries preserved what was historically worth knowing.

33:46.520 --> 33:51.700
Local newspapers, for all their flaws, established what was visible and important in a specific community.

33:52.060 --> 33:54.540
Universities decided what counted as rigorous knowledge.

33:54.540 --> 34:02.660
Even local community groups, churches, and union halls maintained a kind of tacit shared expertise about what was real.

34:02.820 --> 34:03.180
Exactly.

34:03.440 --> 34:04.380
And let's be clear.

34:04.520 --> 34:06.720
None of these legacy institutions were perfect.

34:07.120 --> 34:08.260
They were heavily biased.

34:08.500 --> 34:09.600
They failed often.

34:09.900 --> 34:12.600
They had massive blind spots regarding race and class.

34:12.640 --> 34:13.240
Oh, for sure.

34:13.460 --> 34:17.000
But, and this is the crucial structural point, they were distributed.

34:17.000 --> 34:21.180
They were not all governed by the exact same optimization gradient.

34:21.920 --> 34:28.360
A librarian in Ohio had a different metric for success than a newspaper editor in London or a professor in Tokyo.

34:28.740 --> 34:30.700
The ecosystem had redundancy.

34:31.140 --> 34:35.460
But today, that entire diverse ecology of distinctions has been centralized.

34:36.260 --> 34:38.500
Google search algorithms determine what is knowable.

34:39.060 --> 34:41.960
TikTok and YouTube recommendation engines determine what is visible.

34:42.140 --> 34:43.700
A single optimization gradient.

34:43.700 --> 34:46.440
Almost always maximizing engagement or ad revenue, right?

34:46.780 --> 34:52.320
It now rules the entire process of how our global civilization constructs its shared reality.

34:52.680 --> 34:56.440
The danger isn't just that an algorithm occasionally promotes a piece of misinformation.

34:57.660 --> 35:12.360
The profound structural danger is that our entire civilization's distinction repair machinery has been coupled to a tiny set of corporate metrics that have absolutely no mathematical care for the long-term structural coherence of society.

35:12.360 --> 35:14.200
Because it's a global system now.

35:14.640 --> 35:17.400
When a local library fails, you lose distinction locally.

35:17.920 --> 35:20.380
The town might suffer, but the state survives.

35:20.840 --> 35:30.840
But when a global search engine successfully optimizes its metric at the expense of epistemic integrity, the degradation of truth is global, instantaneous, and unavoidable.

35:31.040 --> 35:32.760
The power isn't in the data they own.

35:33.060 --> 35:34.640
It's in the pathways they control.

35:35.340 --> 35:39.900
Which perfectly brings in the work of Cory Doctorow and his concept of chokepoint capitalism.

35:39.900 --> 35:43.580
Doctorow's insight is perfectly aligned with Fliction's geometric model.

35:44.220 --> 35:47.700
Power in the modern economy often resembles physical mountain passes.

35:47.940 --> 35:48.620
Mountain passes.

35:48.820 --> 35:52.760
Think of a toll bridge over a river or a narrow strait in the ocean.

35:53.300 --> 36:01.220
The most powerful, most profitable firms today don't necessarily produce the goods or create the content or write the software.

36:01.720 --> 36:06.120
They just occupy the choke points through which all that activity must pass.

36:06.120 --> 36:08.820
Oh, like Apple and Google's app stores.

36:08.940 --> 36:12.860
They govern how software gets distributed to billions of people.

36:13.400 --> 36:16.060
Visa and MasterCard govern how we transact.

36:16.640 --> 36:20.160
Amazon Web Services governs the actual computation of the internet.

36:20.380 --> 36:21.400
They don't make the stuff.

36:21.480 --> 36:22.720
They just govern the passage.

36:23.200 --> 36:29.840
And I was reading about a local business recently, a family-owned hardware store that relied entirely on Google Maps for foot traffic.

36:29.840 --> 36:33.560
Google updated its algorithm to prioritize big box stores.

36:34.060 --> 36:37.460
And overnight, this family business lost 60% of its revenue.

36:37.780 --> 36:38.520
That's devastating.

36:38.800 --> 36:39.740
They didn't do anything wrong.

36:39.820 --> 36:40.760
Their products were still good.

36:40.920 --> 36:45.500
But the pathway to their future was instantly erased by the entity controlling the chokepoint.

36:45.660 --> 36:47.580
That is a devastatingly perfect example.

36:47.980 --> 36:54.560
And by concentrating reachability into a few privileged channels like that, these monopolies destroy pathway redundancy.

36:54.720 --> 36:55.680
Back to the chessboard.

36:55.860 --> 36:57.240
Think back to our chessboard.

36:57.240 --> 37:03.180
In a healthy, distributed system, there are many paths to reach a desired future state.

37:03.600 --> 37:05.380
If one route is blocked, you take another.

37:05.960 --> 37:10.820
But a chokepoint collapses all those trajectories into one governed passage.

37:10.940 --> 37:12.100
Which makes it fragile.

37:12.400 --> 37:14.360
It looks incredibly efficient on paper.

37:14.540 --> 37:17.220
It's very easy to manage one app store instead of 50.

37:17.800 --> 37:23.540
But it means a single point of failure can collapse the admissibility volume for millions of people simultaneously.

37:23.540 --> 37:28.320
So big tech and chokepoint capitalism are governing the paths we can take.

37:28.760 --> 37:29.700
They control the roads.

37:30.440 --> 37:36.320
But fliction pairs this with another mechanism that governs the destinations we are allowed to reach in the first place.

37:36.900 --> 37:43.000
And this brings in the work of economic historian Clara Maté on what she calls austerity governance.

37:43.000 --> 37:47.060
Now, we need to be very clear here and maintain strict impartiality.

37:47.420 --> 37:51.720
The caper is discussing political economy through a neutral, structural, and mathematical lens.

37:51.760 --> 37:51.880
Right.

37:51.940 --> 37:53.180
It's not taking a partisan side.

37:53.280 --> 37:53.380
Yeah.

37:53.440 --> 38:11.900
According to Matty's historical analysis, austerity policies, things like harsh budget cuts to social services, wage suppression, the privatization of public assets, strict fiscal discipline, are usually pitched to the voting public as objective, unavoidable fiscal necessity.

38:11.900 --> 38:14.460
Right. The politicians get up and say, you know, we have to tighten our belts.

38:14.540 --> 38:16.880
We have to balance the checkbook like a responsible household.

38:17.380 --> 38:20.600
There simply is no money for the bridge repair or the library.

38:21.260 --> 38:31.000
But Maté argues that historically, austerity functions not just as a neutral economic accounting tool, but as a mechanism to forcefully contract the reachable options space.

38:31.820 --> 38:37.520
Specifically, she argues, it has historically been used to restore or protect social hierarchies when they are threatened.

38:38.220 --> 38:39.520
How does that work structurally?

38:39.660 --> 38:40.680
Let's look at the mechanism.

38:40.680 --> 38:51.700
If a government deliberately removes the fiscal slack that labor movements rely on by cutting unemployment benefits or striking funds, you structurally constrain their ability to bargain for better conditions.

38:51.800 --> 38:53.120
Because they can't afford to strike.

38:53.420 --> 38:55.540
You aren't just saving the government money.

38:56.420 --> 39:04.580
You are structurally removing certain economic futures, like a future with stronger labor rights, entirely off the board.

39:04.580 --> 39:11.620
Let me try to map this onto a hypothetical scenario like a city council meeting to see if I understand the mechanism affliction is describing.

39:11.980 --> 39:14.660
Let's say a city council has to choose between two options.

39:15.360 --> 39:24.700
They can fund the repair of a critical public bridge that connects a working class neighborhood to the city center, or they can offer a massive tax break to attract a corporate warehouse.

39:25.700 --> 39:32.460
Under austerity governance, the metric they are required to optimize is short-term fiscal balance and business friendliness.

39:32.460 --> 39:34.020
Correct. That is their M.

39:34.760 --> 39:38.820
So when they look at the bridge repair, the austerity metric only sees a cost.

39:39.420 --> 39:40.540
It sees a drain on the budget.

39:41.040 --> 39:45.680
It is completely blind to the unoptimizable reality that the bridge allows nurses to get to the hospital,

39:45.940 --> 39:51.120
or allows children to get to school, or simply maintains the structural coherence of the community.

39:51.560 --> 39:54.960
So they cut the bridge funding, and they give the tax break to the warehouse.

39:54.960 --> 40:00.060
Infliction translates this exact scenario into the math of the carelessness principle.

40:01.060 --> 40:09.880
Under an austerity framework, the system is institutionally forced to adopt an accounting metric where the care for repair workers goes to absolute zero.

40:10.740 --> 40:15.940
Mathematically, it's partial M over partial X subrepair approaches zero.

40:15.940 --> 40:18.720
The metric is blind to the necessity of maintenance.

40:18.840 --> 40:19.160
Exactly.

40:19.820 --> 40:20.040
Yes.

40:20.560 --> 40:29.540
Because maintenance workers, public health inspectors, archivists, transit crews, teachers, these are the people who perform the actual repair of society's substrate.

40:30.040 --> 40:31.880
They keep the bridge from falling down.

40:32.180 --> 40:34.620
They keep the water pipes functioning in that second village.

40:34.640 --> 40:35.960
They do the essential work.

40:36.200 --> 40:43.760
But because their work doesn't generate short-term liquid return on a quarterly balance sheet, austerity metrics render them economically invisible.

40:43.760 --> 40:47.020
They are viewed purely as liabilities to be cut.

40:47.300 --> 40:49.360
It enforces indifference to the maintenance class.

40:49.840 --> 40:54.680
The optimization gradient ruthlessly eliminates what the gradient cannot mathematically see.

40:54.960 --> 40:55.940
So what does this all mean?

40:56.020 --> 40:59.100
Let me try to synthesize this whole nightmare before we look at the final math.

40:59.180 --> 40:59.620
Go for it.

40:59.840 --> 41:03.980
Choke points govern the paths we can take, destroying our redundancy.

41:04.320 --> 41:06.680
So we rely on single corporate gateways.

41:07.700 --> 41:10.540
Austerity governs the destinations we are allowed to reach,

41:10.540 --> 41:16.060
systematically destroying the maintenance class that keeps our physical and social world from falling apart.

41:16.260 --> 41:17.180
That's a solid summary.

41:17.560 --> 41:20.880
We have an accelerating system that consumes its own repair infrastructure,

41:21.280 --> 41:24.020
is mathematically blind to the damage it's doing,

41:24.220 --> 41:27.500
and forces us through narrower and narrower pathways.

41:28.240 --> 41:29.480
Where does this end?

41:29.780 --> 41:31.380
What is the bottom of this well?

41:31.600 --> 41:34.180
This is where we arrive at the most intense part of the paper,

41:34.560 --> 41:37.200
the actual math of civilizational collapse.

41:37.200 --> 41:40.140
Fliction formalizes everything we've discussed,

41:40.580 --> 41:45.420
the metrics, the repair, the destruction, into a master admissibility equation.

41:45.580 --> 41:48.620
Okay, I'm going to introduce this equation, but listener, do not panic.

41:48.980 --> 41:51.620
We are not going to throw a bunch of Greek letters at you all at once.

41:51.760 --> 41:55.580
We're going to walk through what this math actually describes in the real world.

41:55.780 --> 41:57.840
It's actually very intuitive once you break it down.

41:57.980 --> 42:02.280
The core of the equation is trying to measure one thing dvA over dt.

42:02.280 --> 42:10.260
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, dvA over dt simply means the rate of change of our admissible futures over time.

42:10.920 --> 42:12.280
It's asking a simple question.

42:12.960 --> 42:17.380
As time ticks forward, are our societal options expanding or are they shrinking?

42:18.000 --> 42:20.740
Is the chessboard getting bigger or is it getting smaller?

42:21.100 --> 42:24.940
Let's look at the positive forces first, the things keeping us alive.

42:25.200 --> 42:28.760
There's a baseline repair rate, which is just the normal institutional maintenance.

42:28.760 --> 42:31.980
But then the paper highlights this really interesting term.

42:32.300 --> 42:34.280
It calls it the sigmoidal repair floor.

42:34.880 --> 42:37.140
What is that and why is it sigmoidal?

42:37.440 --> 42:39.800
A sigmoidal function looks like an S-curve on a graph.

42:40.120 --> 42:42.960
It starts flat, rises sharply, and then levels off.

42:43.460 --> 42:47.820
In this model, the sigmoidal repair floor represents autonomous human repair.

42:48.500 --> 42:49.600
What does that mean in practice?

42:49.880 --> 42:53.680
This is the unoptimizable, deeply human work that doesn't show up in GDP.

42:54.160 --> 42:56.820
It's the volunteers organizing a neighborhood watch.

42:56.820 --> 43:00.380
It's the parents staying up late to tutor their kids when the school fails.

43:00.520 --> 43:01.860
It's the community organizers.

43:02.160 --> 43:03.260
The people just helping out.

43:03.460 --> 43:09.240
It's the guy running a local open source server out of his beastment just because he believes in a free internet.

43:09.600 --> 43:13.540
It's all the people who repair society's distinctions just because they care,

43:13.720 --> 43:16.460
not because an optimization metric is paying them to do it.

43:16.500 --> 43:19.080
It's the PTA moms and the mutual aid groups.

43:19.220 --> 43:19.420
Yeah.

43:19.740 --> 43:25.460
They are the ones holding the crumbling infrastructure together with duct tape, sheer willpower, and goodwill.

43:25.460 --> 43:29.000
Exactly. And the S-curve shape is mathematically crucial.

43:29.220 --> 43:29.480
Why?

43:29.980 --> 43:35.200
It means that for a very long time, as the formal system degrades and the pipes start to leak,

43:35.660 --> 43:41.120
this autonomous human repair acts as a strong, persistent break against total collapse.

43:41.400 --> 43:43.200
The community absorbs the shock.

43:43.480 --> 43:45.900
The state fails to provide, so the community steps up.

43:46.220 --> 43:48.460
The S-curve rises to meet the challenge.

43:48.800 --> 43:53.440
But, and this is the terrifying part of the math, there is a limit to human endurance.

43:53.600 --> 43:54.100
It burnt out.

43:54.100 --> 43:59.940
If the option space shrinks too much, if it nears a critical threshold that Fliction calls V-subcrit,

44:00.360 --> 44:02.600
this human break loses its substrate.

44:03.020 --> 44:05.860
Meaning, the people holding it together just get too exhausted.

44:06.440 --> 44:07.860
The PTA moms burn out.

44:08.440 --> 44:10.140
The open source guy runs out of money.

44:10.480 --> 44:17.900
The social trust they rely on to organize a mutual aid network completely evaporates because everyone is too desperate just trying to survive.

44:17.900 --> 44:22.600
Yes. The underlying conditions required for voluntary repair vanish.

44:23.140 --> 44:26.360
And when that happens, the S-curve suddenly drops off a cliff.

44:26.660 --> 44:27.580
It goes to zero.

44:27.960 --> 44:29.100
The human break fails.

44:29.500 --> 44:30.220
And what happens then?

44:30.400 --> 44:34.380
When that break fails, the negative terms in the equation take absolute control.

44:34.840 --> 44:38.400
The paper models this as irreversible distinction loss.

44:38.400 --> 44:47.460
The math here shows that as our options narrow, as the volume of our futures gets smaller, the rate of destruction actually accelerates.

44:47.540 --> 44:48.460
It's a feedback loop.

44:48.580 --> 44:48.960
It is.

44:49.180 --> 44:51.360
As systems lose options, they become desperate.

44:51.900 --> 44:54.540
They over-specialize just to survive the next quarter.

44:54.660 --> 44:56.580
They start cannibalizing their own foundations.

44:56.580 --> 44:57.820
We see this historically.

44:58.180 --> 44:58.400
Like where?

44:58.760 --> 45:02.560
When a society is under extreme stress, languages die faster.

45:03.060 --> 45:05.520
Ecological species go extinct rapidly.

45:06.160 --> 45:13.380
Institutional memory evaporates instantly when the elder generation retires because there was no fiscal slack left to train their successors.

45:13.760 --> 45:15.060
The knowledge is just gone.

45:15.480 --> 45:19.500
So we have the human break failing and the destruction accelerating simultaneously.

45:19.500 --> 45:27.260
And Flicken says this moment, this threshold, is the literal mathematical definition of civilizational collapse.

45:27.720 --> 45:28.500
It's not a metaphor.

45:28.820 --> 45:29.640
It's a phase transition.

45:29.860 --> 45:34.900
It's reaching that critical threshold, V subcrit, where the system can no longer repair itself internally.

45:35.460 --> 45:40.980
It falls into a state where it would require a discontinuous, massive external intervention to be saved.

45:41.140 --> 45:45.200
The paper notes that historically societies rarely see this threshold coming.

45:45.200 --> 45:50.560
They cite the late Roman Empire or the collapse of the Soviet administrative system in the late 1980s.

45:50.620 --> 45:51.660
It triefs up on you.

45:51.940 --> 45:57.140
In both cases, the collapse of the underlying repair infrastructure happened slowly, over decades.

45:57.480 --> 46:00.220
People just got used to things working a little bit worse every year.

46:00.400 --> 46:04.880
And the actual mathematical threshold of no return was only recognized in the rearview mirror.

46:05.240 --> 46:10.500
But the paper issues a massive flashing red warning sign right here.

46:10.500 --> 46:17.500
And I think this is one of the most important insights in the whole piece, especially for the political era we live in now.

46:18.960 --> 46:21.340
Fliction calls it the emergency paradox.

46:22.180 --> 46:25.560
This brings us right back to Baudrillard and the map eating the territory.

46:26.260 --> 46:33.640
Fliction warns us, mathematically, to be incredibly wary of political figures, particularly authoritarians,

46:33.820 --> 46:37.840
who loudly proclaim that we have already hit the collapse threshold.

46:37.840 --> 46:41.800
Because what do authoritarians do when they claim there is an existential emergency?

46:42.140 --> 46:47.260
They use the narrative of collapse to justify suspending whatever democratic processes are left.

46:47.320 --> 46:47.620
Exactly.

46:47.820 --> 46:50.900
They get up on a podium and say, you know, the system is too broken for debate.

46:51.060 --> 46:52.100
The parliament is too slow.

46:52.200 --> 46:53.100
The courts are too inefficient.

46:53.580 --> 46:57.000
I must take absolute unilateral control to save you from the chaos.

46:57.180 --> 47:02.640
And by doing so, by declaring the emergency, they deliberately close off the remaining political pathways.

47:02.640 --> 47:10.360
The representation of the crisis, the political narrative that we are collapsing, actually causes the final fatal contraction of the option space.

47:10.660 --> 47:13.220
The map of the emergency triggers the real world collapse.

47:13.580 --> 47:14.880
Fliction puts it beautifully.

47:15.640 --> 47:20.980
A framework designed to expand futures cannot be coherently used to justify foreclosing them.

47:21.420 --> 47:24.900
You cannot save the chessboard by burning half the squares.

47:25.120 --> 47:25.760
That is chilling.

47:26.100 --> 47:30.660
It's a structural warning about the seduction of strongmen in a decaying system.

47:30.660 --> 47:34.640
But we promised the listener we weren't just going to be doomsday prophets here.

47:35.420 --> 47:37.560
The master equation isn't just about collapse.

47:37.720 --> 47:39.260
It has positive terms.

47:39.460 --> 47:40.740
Yes, there is a way out.

47:40.900 --> 47:42.480
It has terms for structural repair.

47:42.780 --> 47:47.460
And it has a fascinating term called N of T for the generation of novelty.

47:47.940 --> 47:50.300
So how do we actually fix the math?

47:50.640 --> 47:53.500
How do we force a blind system to see the damage it's doing?

47:53.600 --> 47:56.980
How do we expand the option space before we hit the threshold?

47:56.980 --> 48:01.840
The beauty of Fliction's proposed solutions is that they aren't framed as moral imperatives.

48:02.300 --> 48:07.340
They aren't asking the CEOs of massive tech monopolies or factory farms to suddenly wake up and become saints.

48:07.580 --> 48:10.420
They recognize that shame doesn't work on a partial derivative.

48:10.680 --> 48:11.540
So what does work?

48:11.720 --> 48:15.360
Instead, the solutions are framed as structural necessities to fix the math.

48:15.740 --> 48:17.960
We have to change the optimization gradients.

48:18.200 --> 48:20.940
Let's go through the key interventions the paper suggests.

48:20.940 --> 48:24.420
First, internalizing repair costs.

48:25.260 --> 48:31.040
If the problem is that the metric M is blind to the substrate X, we have to force the metric to see it.

48:31.380 --> 48:31.660
Exactly.

48:32.200 --> 48:38.220
Let's look at policies like aggressive carbon pricing or extended producer responsibility legislation,

48:38.580 --> 48:44.240
which legally forces a manufacturer to pay for the recycling of their product at the end of its life.

48:44.520 --> 48:47.500
Or mandatory social impact accounting.

48:47.940 --> 48:49.740
Which people usually debate politically.

48:49.740 --> 48:55.440
In our current political discourse, these are usually debated as if they are moral punishments for bad corporations.

48:56.260 --> 48:57.780
Fliction says, no, look at the geometry.

48:57.960 --> 48:59.580
These are purely structural corrections.

48:59.780 --> 49:00.140
Oh, so.

49:00.380 --> 49:04.400
They reduce the mathematical distance between the metric and the underlying reality.

49:05.260 --> 49:09.940
If a smartphone company legally has to pay for the ecological repair of its rare earth mining,

49:10.420 --> 49:13.500
suddenly partial M over partial X is no longer zero.

49:13.720 --> 49:14.540
The gradient shifts.

49:14.680 --> 49:15.440
The gradient shifts.

49:15.440 --> 49:19.920
The system suddenly cares about the river because the river is now legally on the balance sheet.

49:20.240 --> 49:23.260
It's just fixing the accounting so the map actually reflects the territory.

49:23.860 --> 49:26.200
The second intervention is restoring redundancy.

49:26.700 --> 49:30.540
This is the direct structural antidote to chokepoint capitalism.

49:31.140 --> 49:35.140
If chokepoints compress our reachability by forcing everyone through a single toll booth,

49:35.140 --> 49:39.840
we need policies that actively mandate and maintain alternative routes.

49:39.960 --> 49:40.960
Breaking up some monopolies.

49:40.960 --> 49:44.960
This means aggressive antitrust enforcement to break up monopolies.

49:45.580 --> 49:53.220
It means mandatory interoperability between digital platforms so you can message someone on WhatsApp from a completely different independent app.

49:53.300 --> 49:54.160
Oh, that would be amazing.

49:54.160 --> 49:57.300
It means open protocol mandates for critical infrastructure.

49:57.940 --> 50:04.400
We must ensure that our civilization does not rely on a single private optimized gateway for its core functions.

50:04.860 --> 50:06.700
We need redundant bridges.

50:07.220 --> 50:11.800
And the third intervention, which really resonated with me because it feels so culturally backward right now,

50:11.860 --> 50:13.620
is rebuilding the maintenance class.

50:14.120 --> 50:20.380
Our current civilization relentlessly rewards creation and disruption, and it entirely ignores maintenance.

50:20.380 --> 50:25.620
We put founders, disruptors, and venture capitalists on magazine covers.

50:25.900 --> 50:26.640
Right, the innovators.

50:26.880 --> 50:32.720
But the people who actually maintain the admissibility of our world, the public school teachers, the public health workers,

50:33.240 --> 50:38.560
the transit crews, the sanitation workers, the archivists, are economically invisible.

50:39.520 --> 50:44.480
Under austerity metrics, they are viewed strictly as cost centers to be optimized away.

50:44.480 --> 50:50.940
To fix the equation, the paper argues we have to fundamentally change what counts as productive economic activity.

50:51.320 --> 50:57.740
We have to redirect capital to invest heavily in the messy, slow, unoptimizable work of repair.

50:58.260 --> 50:59.140
But here's a question.

50:59.600 --> 51:01.880
Repair only gets us back to where we started, right?

51:01.940 --> 51:02.920
Technically, yes.

51:02.940 --> 51:05.300
It just restores the degraded options we already had.

51:05.380 --> 51:06.700
What about creating new futures?

51:06.920 --> 51:08.640
How do we get out of the rut entirely?

51:09.080 --> 51:11.300
That's the N of T term in the equation, right?

51:11.640 --> 51:12.820
The generation of novelty.

51:12.820 --> 51:19.660
Yes, N of T represents the rate at which genuinely new, previously unreachable, admissible pathways are opened.

51:20.180 --> 51:24.840
And this is crucial because we can't just repair our way out of a fundamentally flawed paradigm.

51:25.180 --> 51:27.120
We have to innovate structurally.

51:27.340 --> 51:29.060
Here's where it gets really interesting.

51:29.660 --> 51:40.200
Fliction points out that historically, the massive civilization-saving expansions of human option space almost never came from the center of power.

51:40.280 --> 51:41.240
No, they rarely do.

51:41.240 --> 51:48.900
The center, the massive corporations, the federal governments, the elite universities, they are too trapped in their own optimization gradients.

51:49.460 --> 51:52.240
The novelty, the N of T, came from the periphery.

51:53.000 --> 51:56.540
The paper identifies three main sources of this systemic novelty.

51:57.060 --> 51:59.240
First, crisis-generated reorganization.

52:00.220 --> 52:05.880
Think of the New Deal in the United States during the Great Depression or the massive post-war reconstruction of Western Europe.

52:06.080 --> 52:07.500
What happens in those moments?

52:07.500 --> 52:18.940
Sometimes, a crisis discredits the dominant optimization landscape so thoroughly that society suddenly gains the political slack to completely redesign the architecture of its institutions from scratch.

52:18.940 --> 52:25.020
It's a high-risk way to get novelty because the crisis has to be devastating, but historically, it does force a reset.

52:25.460 --> 52:26.420
What's the second source?

52:26.420 --> 52:28.740
The second is institutional mutation.

52:28.740 --> 52:35.200
This occurs when existing legacy institutions somehow manage to defy the gravity of their own capture.

52:35.700 --> 52:45.000
For example, when a regulatory agency, against all odds, actually enforces strict rules against the wealthy industry that funds its political overlords.

52:45.000 --> 52:52.700
Or when a conservative legal system generates a radical precedent that protects the vulnerable and constrains the powerful.

52:52.700 --> 52:54.940
Those rare, surprising wins.

52:55.780 --> 53:05.120
These mutations happen when institutional cultures manage to defend a pocket of autonomy, a little walled garden of slack away from the dominant metric.

53:05.120 --> 53:11.120
And the third source, which I think might be the most actionable for us and for the listener right now, is peripheral experimentation.

53:11.900 --> 53:12.100
Yes.

53:12.540 --> 53:20.680
The dominant optimization landscape, the massive tech monopolies and austerity governments, does not cover every single inch of social space.

53:21.080 --> 53:24.220
At the margins, in the cracks of the pavement, people are experimenting.

53:24.400 --> 53:25.400
Like in open source software.

53:25.400 --> 53:26.120
Exactly.

53:26.540 --> 53:35.480
In open source software ecosystems, in local mutual aid networks, in worker-owned cooperative enterprises, and small-scale radical local governance models,

53:36.060 --> 53:43.940
people out on the periphery are actively figuring out alternative ways to repair and maintain society completely outside the primary profit gradient.

53:44.140 --> 53:44.960
So they serve as a model.

53:45.260 --> 53:48.440
These peripheries act as crucial existence proofs.

53:48.860 --> 53:53.620
They prove that alternative, structurally sound futures are still mathematically reachable.

53:53.620 --> 53:58.280
So how do we bring these peripheral experiments to the center?

53:58.420 --> 54:04.780
How do we actually shift the momentum of the entire civilization before the human break fails?

54:05.100 --> 54:09.300
Flick Sheen argues that the only way to scale this is to build repair coalitions.

54:09.760 --> 54:18.020
And the fascinating part, and I think the most hopeful part of the entire paper, is that these coalitions absolutely do not require ideological purity.

54:18.020 --> 54:25.940
Right. You don't need everyone in the coalition to agree on a perfect, unified, utopian vision of what the future should look like.

54:26.000 --> 54:32.480
Exactly. Think of a political coalition fighting for right to repair legislation for electronics and farm equipment.

54:32.580 --> 54:33.160
That's a great example.

54:33.300 --> 54:39.140
Inside that coalition, you might have radical environmentalists motivated strictly by reducing toxic e-waste.

54:39.500 --> 54:47.420
You might have conservative, multi-generational farmers motivated by wanting the independents to fix their own tractors without paying a corporate technician.

54:47.420 --> 54:49.220
And they both want the same policy.

54:49.600 --> 54:57.640
You might also have progressive consumer advocates fighting monopolies and libertarian small business owners wanting to open independent repair shops.

54:58.100 --> 55:02.800
They all have completely different personal metrics. They have different political philosophies.

55:02.900 --> 55:04.220
But they share the geometry.

55:04.620 --> 55:09.020
Structurally, they all benefit from expanding the option space rather than shrinking it.

55:09.400 --> 55:12.200
They are united by the geometry of the problem.

55:12.200 --> 55:14.780
They don't need to agree on how to play the game.

55:15.120 --> 55:19.220
They just need to agree that the chessboard needs to get bigger, not smaller.

55:20.200 --> 55:22.480
They are united against the choke point.

55:22.700 --> 55:23.060
Precisely.

55:23.460 --> 55:26.060
Okay. We have covered an immense amount of ground today.

55:26.540 --> 55:28.960
Let's try to summarize this massive intellectual journey.

55:29.760 --> 55:33.960
We started with the overwhelming feeling that the world is running out of resources.

55:34.600 --> 55:36.180
The standard scarcity narrative.

55:36.300 --> 55:37.400
And we saw that was a trap.

55:37.400 --> 55:43.720
But Fliction's brilliant paper has shown us that the meta-crisis isn't about running out of stuff.

55:44.180 --> 55:49.080
It's about our hyper-optimized systems systematically consuming the pathways to tomorrow.

55:49.320 --> 56:00.780
We explored how local rational optimization driven by the carelessness principle mathematically blinds our institutions to the damage they do to the physical and social substrate of reality.

56:00.960 --> 56:02.440
The truck ignoring the road.

56:02.440 --> 56:12.000
We saw how the compression trap forces us to navigate by simplified metrics that no longer reflect the territory, and how platforms and choke points centralize and restrict our movement.

56:12.420 --> 56:19.440
And we looked at the literal math of collapse, where the human break of voluntary repair fails, and the destruction of our institutions accelerates.

56:20.120 --> 56:21.880
But we also saw the math of expansion.

56:21.880 --> 56:30.740
Internalizing costs to force the metric to see the damage, restoring redundancy to bypass the choke points, and radically funding the maintenance class.

56:31.160 --> 56:33.580
The fix isn't just finding a better metric to optimize.

56:34.160 --> 56:37.500
The fix is a fundamental reinvestment in the unoptimizable.

56:37.960 --> 56:39.660
We must change what counts as productive.

56:39.660 --> 56:48.460
We have to bring the slow, careful work of maintenance, of distinction preservation, of structural repair back inside the accounting boundary.

56:48.680 --> 56:53.480
Because if we don't, the gradient will just keep pointing toward contraction until the options run out.

56:53.480 --> 56:58.680
And that brings me to a final, provocative thought I want to leave you, the listener, with today.

56:59.240 --> 57:08.100
We've talked for an hour about civilizational scale, about global tech monopolies, macroeconomic austerity, and mathematical equations governing society.

57:08.400 --> 57:09.660
But look at your own life.

57:10.060 --> 57:13.460
Look at the personal metrics you use to govern yourself every single day.

57:13.540 --> 57:15.040
It applies on the micro level, too.

57:15.040 --> 57:16.580
Look at your screen time app.

57:17.000 --> 57:21.680
Look at your bank account balance, or your GPA, or the rings on your fitness tracker.

57:22.080 --> 57:29.600
Ask yourself, are those metrics actually capturing the messy, unoptimizable, complex reality of your actual well-being?

57:30.200 --> 57:33.800
Or are you exhausting yourself trying to repair the representation?

57:34.520 --> 57:40.960
Are you trying to make the numbers on the screen look good, while the substrate of your actual physical and mental life quietly degrades in the background?

57:41.440 --> 57:43.480
That is the perfect question to ask.

57:43.480 --> 57:49.360
Look, the map is not the territory in our macro institutions, but it isn't the territory in our personal lives, either.

57:49.620 --> 57:50.020
Exactly.

57:50.360 --> 57:51.540
And ask yourself this, too.

57:52.340 --> 57:57.020
Where in your own life can you step outside the optimization gradient?

57:57.620 --> 58:01.220
Where can you be part of the maintenance class today in your own neighborhood?

58:01.760 --> 58:04.840
Can you contribute to a local, unoptimized project?

58:05.300 --> 58:07.480
Can you support a physical community space?

58:07.480 --> 58:14.060
Can you just be fiercely protective of a friendship or a relationship that has absolutely zero measurable return on investment?

58:14.500 --> 58:16.400
Because that is how we build the S-curve.

58:16.640 --> 58:19.560
That is how we rebuild the village pipes so the water can flow again.

58:19.680 --> 58:21.780
It's the only way we keep the future admissible.

58:22.020 --> 58:24.340
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

58:24.900 --> 58:25.940
Keep questioning the map.

58:26.300 --> 58:27.320
Keep repairing the territory.

58:27.880 --> 58:29.000
And we will see you next time.

58:29.000 --> 58:58.980
We'll see you next time.

